A||N||11||DaveL||87 SE Owner Explodes||||8||
Z||000000||DaveL||03-13-2000||08:47 AM||||Well, I finally did it. The check eng soon light has been lit for months, code 14. A mechanic has replaced the ECM in my 87 SE 2.5 DIS. I tracked the problem down to the connection at the ECM. Weirdly though, the plugs connect on the right side of this ECM instead of on the bottom as shown in my Haynes manual. At any rate, when I press back on the top connector, the light goes out and it runs GREAT !I took the plug out and sprayed electric contact cleaner on both the plug and the ECM conn., no dice. So I put a plastic strap around the ECM and the connector in question which helped but it still happens occasionally. The mechanic has already replaced the plug I think. Could this just be a faulty ECM? I can't believe he couldn't fix this. A plastic strap is a pretty cheasy fix, even for me. Perhaps some sort of miniature wire brush for the plug or something. It looks like the plug comes apart easy enough, red insert in a black plug, but I really don't want to take it apart unless I have to.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Raydar||03-13-2000||10:51 AM||||Dave, it could be a bad solder connection where the connector (on the ECM side) is soldered to the circuit board. Been a while since I had one apart, so I can't tell you where to look. If you decide to chase this, be careful. They are probably very sensitive to static electricity. Might pay you to find a "recycled" ECM. They can be found pretty cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-13-2000||04:10 PM||||Since it did it on 2 ECM's it's posible it the harness connector. Could have a bad spring terminal in it. I think you can get new terminals for that connector, but I'm not sure.<P>Yes, the ECM plug can be shelled or just a single terminal replaced. You carefully dig out the red wedge, and that unlocks the terminals.<P>I haven't looked at my 87's ECM in a long time. I don't remember if they are "edge" connector or pins. Sounds like a cracked solder joint on a pin. Not hard to fix if you have a decent iron. Its easy enough to crack a pin loose so it's entirely posible both ECMs are messed up.<P>Allot of things for 87+ 4 cyl changed. Don't count on Haynes or Chilton for them.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaveL||03-15-2000||09:03 AM||||Thanks, I'm going to try and determine which one is bad, but if I pry the pink insert out of the harness I hope all the terminals don't just fall out in my hand. I agree, since it did the same thing with two ECMs, I'm sure the problem lies in the plug itself.||||reg||10
Z||000004||DRH||03-15-2000||01:49 PM||||The red cover just kind of keeps tension off the wires going into the connector. Each wire is held into the connector by a little "tab".<P>The wire causing you trouble is probably a Yellow wire (return from temp sensor). On all V6 and pre 87 4 cyl it is terminal 10. Not sure if position changed for 87-88 4 cyl...||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaveL||03-15-2000||02:42 PM||||There is indeed a yellow wire coming from the sensor. I got a diagram for the ecm connectors off the net, and it does say that it's wire #10 of the larger of the two. This is on the smaller of the two plugs. They obviously knew I was destined to buy an 87, and so changed everything just to confuse me. Thanks for the reassurance about the connector, I can almost see myself sitting there holding a jumbled handfull of wires in one hand and the end of a plug in the other. I'll try the plug very carefully and see what it looks like. ||||reg||5
Z||000006||DRH||03-15-2000||03:53 PM||||Don't worry... all the wires won't fall out. In fact, you will probably have trouble removing the one wire.<P>A metal connector is crimped on to the end of each wire. This connector is pushed in to the plastic connector and has a small tab on the metal part that locks it in. There are tools made for removing them. Usually you can get them out with a <B>very</B> small flat screw driver and a little finesse. Just don't pull on the wire too hard. It will slide right out when the tab is released.<P>Besides being dirty... check for a bent connector (the metal part crimped on the wire) or a bad connection where it is crimped on the wire.<P>||||reg||7
Z||000007||DaveL||03-16-2000||08:34 AM||||One other question before I try this. Is it OK to unplug these connectors as long as the ignition switch is off, or do you absolutely have to unhook the battery? That is a kind of a pain. Also, if I need to do any solder should I use acid core or resin core solder? Chees, I guess thats two questions. Oops<p>[This message has been edited by DaveL (edited 03-16-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000008||theogre||03-16-2000||11:52 AM||||Resin Core...... Always Resin core for all electrical work. Acid is Only for use in plumbing and body work. Even then resin is prefered most of the time. (Most current resins are water based cleanup. Acid flux must be nutralized as well as cleaned off.)<P>The battery must be disconnected. These connectors are hot even if the key is off.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaveL||03-27-2000||09:00 AM||||Hooray, It's finally fixed. Took both plugs apart, really quite easy if you have the right tool. There was nothing wrong with the plugs or wiring harness at all. The original "mechanic" that I took it to put in two rotten used ECMs. That was all smoke and mirrors to cover this guys butt. I put in a remanufactured ECM that I got from my local GM dealer for $175 with the prom from the old unit, no more check engine soon light, runs like a top. Any one who reads this, don't ever buy used ECM units !!! Turned out the one he put in had bad solder joints where the connector meets the circuit board. Also put in a set of really good 4"X10" speakers in the dash, and the difference is unbelievable. Now I can tackle the air conditioning and the usual front end trouble with a car this old. Here's something wierd about this car. I thought the odometer was messed up, but now I realize that all my guages are metric, and I think the odometer is reading kilometers instead of miles. Anyone ever heard of this? My inoperative temp guage reads in Centigrade, oil pressure in some units I never saw before. Think it was made in Canada.||||reg||10
Z||000010||theogre||03-27-2000||01:08 PM||||Metric would likely mean it was for Canadian sale. The oil pressure is Killo Pascals if I remember correctly.<P>You can put in the U.S. dash if you want. I don't believe you have to do anything but drop in the new dash. The diferances are all cosmetic.<P>I'd like to have a look at that bad ECM if you don't want it.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaveL||03-27-2000||04:04 PM||||I'd like to have looked at it more myself, but there was a LARGE core charge, so I handed it in when I picked up the new unit. It was a used GM ECM.<P>Now on to bigger and other problems!! I'll have to do the conversion to check my actual mileage.||||reg||10
